{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Under Rule 12d1-4, before acquiring shares beyond statutory limits, the acquiring fund’s adviser (or subadviser) generally must determine that the arrangement is in the acquiring fund’s best interests, considering fees and complexity, and that it will not overreach or otherwise disadvantage the acquiring fund. The acquired fund’s adviser must find that the arrangement is in the acquired fund’s best interests, considering any reasonably foreseeable effects on scale, including potential economies of scale, and that it will not overreach or disadvantage the acquired fund.\n\nThe funds’ advisers generally must enter into a **fund-of-funds investment agreement** covering material terms, including the acquiring fund’s investment limits, fee-related conditions, and the circumstances under which the acquired fund may use redemption restrictions. An agreement is not required in certain same-group arrangements, but the rule’s findings and other conditions still apply."}}
